// The deflate package: DEFLATE (RFC 1951) compression and decompression, with
// zlib (RFC 1950) and gzip (RFC 1952) framing, the Adler-32 and CRC-32 they
// need, and streaming in both directions. Written in Cone, with no C library
// and no call of the OS's.
//
//   mod squeeze;
//   import stdio use *;
//   import deflate;
//
//   fn main() i32 {
//     imm packed = deflate.gzipCompress("the quick brown fox", 2);   // a gzip file in a List[u8]
//     match deflate.gzipDecompress(packed.view(), 0usize) {          // and back
//       case imm ok Ok { printInt(i64.from(ok.len())); }              // 19
//       case imm e Err { printStr(e.reason.view()); return 1i32; }
//     }
//     0i32;
//   }
//
// This package was carved out of imagedecode (which stands on it now: its PNG
// reader and writer call it, and it re-exports 'inflate', 'zlibDecompress',
// 'zlibCompress', 'adler32' and 'Crc32' under the names it always had). Its
// gzip framing, its streaming, and the one-call-per-format helpers are new.
//
// WHAT IS HERE
//
//   ONE CALL         the data in memory, the answer out
//     zlibCompress(data, level) List[u8]          a zlib stream
//     zlibDecompress(data, expected) Result[List[u8], DeflateError]
//     gzipCompress(data, level) List[u8]          one gzip member, a plain header
//     gzipCompressWith(data, level, &header) List[u8]    the same, with a GzipHeader
//     gzipDecompress(data, expected) Result[List[u8], DeflateError]
//     rawDeflate(data, level) List[u8]            the bare DEFLATE stream
//     inflate(data, start, expected, &mut used) Result[List[u8], DeflateError]
//   'expected' is the output size if known (0 if not): it reserves room and is
//   also the most output allowed (0 allows 1 GiB), so a stream that claims to be
//   small and is not is refused as TooLarge. 'level' is 0 to 9 (below).
//   gzipDecompress inflates every member and joins their data, as 'gzip -d' does.
//
//   STREAMING        a value that holds the whole state; any chunking
//     Inflater.make(format)       format is Format.Raw, Format.Zlib or Format.Gzip
//       feed(&Array[u8])          input, any size, split anywhere (copied)
//       finish()                  no more input will come
//       pull(&mut IoBuf) Status   appends into the buffer's spare room, at most
//                                 its room (the Transform of package streams)
//       setLimit(bytes), setMultiMember(bool), error(), gzipHeader(),
//       memberCount(), totalIn(), totalOut(), isDone(), takeRest()
//     Deflater.make(format, level)
//       feed, finish, pull as above; setGzipHeader(&header), totalIn(),
//       totalOut(), isDone()
//     Status (package streams', one for every transform): NeedInput (use up
//     what you have: feed more or finish), MoreOutput (pull again), Done, Failed
//     (an Inflater only; 'error()' says why).
//     Both meet 'streams.Transform' (feed, pull into an IoBuf, finish), so a
//     stage runs either without knowing which.
//     'examples/tour.cone' feeds five bytes at a time and takes eight out.
//
//   CHECKSUMS
//     adler32(data) u32, Adler32 (start, update, value) to go on across pieces
//     crc32(data) u32, Crc32 (make, update(crc, data, from, to)) likewise
//
//   TYPES
//     DeflateError {kind, offset, reason}   kind is an ErrorKind: BadHeader,
//         BadBlock, BadCode, BadDistance, Truncated, BadChecksum, TooLarge,
//         TrailingData; 'offset' is the input bytes used when it was noticed
//     GzipHeader {mtime, xfl, os, text, name, comment, extra}
//
// STREAMING, IN A SENTENCE EACH
//
//   An Inflater decodes one unit at a time (a block header, a table of codes,
//   one symbol with its extra bits and distance). A unit that the input ends
//   inside is put back whole and the pull answers NeedInput; so input can be
//   cut anywhere, a byte at a time, and no unit is half-done. The last symbol
//   of a stream is decoded as soon as its own bits are there, so a raw or zlib
//   stream is Done without 'finish' (a gzip file that may have more members
//   needs 'finish', or 'setMultiMember(false)', to know it is over).
//   Output comes in pieces of at most 'max' bytes, a match cut mid-copy
//   resumes on the next pull, and the Inflater's own memory is the input not
//   yet used plus 32 KiB of history (plus up to 64 KiB of slack), whatever the
//   stream's size. A Deflater turns input into tokens a block at a time, and its
//   bytes depend only on the data, never on how it was cut (a test cuts it
//   five ways at every size and shape and compares).
//
// METHOD CHOICES. These are defaults a later design may revisit. Each says what
// was chosen and what was left out.
//
// - Inflating is zlib's design, as imagedecode had it: a lookup table indexed
//   by the next 10 bits answers every code of that length or less at once, and
//   a longer code falls to the canonical walk of puff.c (Mark Adler). An
//   over-subscribed code is refused; an incomplete one only when it holds one
//   code (RFC 1951 allows a lone distance code), as zlib does. The fixed codes
//   are built when a fixed block first needs them and kept until a dynamic block
//   replaces them.
// - Streaming by putting a unit back, not by a state machine that can stop at
//   any bit (zlib's inflate.c). The cost: the fed input is kept until used (a
//   copy, so the caller's buffer is its own at once), and a dynamic block's
//   table description of up to about 2.5 KiB is read again from its start each
//   time more input arrives before it is complete (fed a byte at a time, that is
//   the table's size squared in bit reads, once per block). The gain: the hot
//   loop is the plain one-shot loop, and the code is a fraction of a bit-level
//   state machine's. The one-shot helpers feed the whole input at once and keep
//   the whole output (so a one-shot inflate does not copy its output).
// - A bomb stopper: output is capped at 1 GiB unless 'setLimit' (or the
//   one-shot 'expected') says otherwise. DEFLATE can expand 1032 to 1, so a
//   limit is the only memory bound a hostile stream leaves. The room reserved
//   up front is never more than 64 MiB on a header's word.
// - The Adler-32 sums 5552 bytes between reductions (the most that cannot
//   overflow 32 bits), as zlib does. The CRC-32 takes four bytes a step
//   ("slicing-by-4", Kounavis and Berry 2005) from a 4 KiB table that a 'Crc32'
//   value holds: made by 'Crc32.make()', no global. 'crc32(data)' makes one each
//   call; keep a Crc32 for many calls. Slicing-by-8 and the carry-less multiply
//   instructions were left out.
// - Deflating: levels 0 to 9, as zlib and miniz_oxide number them. 0 stores, in
//   blocks of 65535. 1 is LZ77 with a hash chain of three-byte strings (32768
//   buckets of the 15 low bits of a shift-xor of the three bytes), one
//   candidate a position, a match entering only its first position in the chain
//   (as miniz's level 1 does), coded with the fixed Huffman code in one block.
//   2 to 9 are the same matcher coded with a dynamic Huffman code per block of
//   32768 tokens, every position a match covers entered in the chain, and these
//   candidates a position: 6, 32, 16, 32, 128, 256, 512, 768 (miniz_oxide's
//   NUM_PROBES for its levels 2 to 9; a probe here is one chain step, miniz's
//   counts groups of up to three).
//   The dynamic code's lengths come from Huffman's merge of the two lightest,
//   limited to 15 bits (7 for the code-length code) by halving the frequencies
//   until they fit, run-length coded with symbols 16, 17 and 18. Levels 1 to 3
//   match greedily (zlib's deflate_fast without its tuning): the longest match
//   in the chain, taken if 3 or more. Levels 4 to 9 match lazily, as miniz's
//   compress_normal does: a match of under 128 bytes is held back one position,
//   and if the next position has a longer match the byte here is written as a
//   literal and the longer one is held in its turn (so a run of improving
//   matches costs a literal each); a match of 128 or more is taken at once.
//   While a held match is 32 or more long the search for a longer one at the
//   next position looks at a quarter of the candidates (miniz's second probe
//   count), and a match of 3 bytes more than 8192 away is dropped (miniz's
//   far_and_small; only the lazy levels do it, so levels 1 to 3 are as they
//   were). No chain limit shrinks after a good match, as zlib's does. The
//   window is 32 KiB. A negative level is zlib's
//   default, 6 (before levels 3 to 9 existed, the default was 2, and any level
//   over 2 acted as 2); a level over 9 acts as 9.
// - A streaming Deflater cannot fall back to stored blocks when the data does
//   not compress (it does not know the end when it writes a block); random
//   data grows a little through it (70000 random bytes make 70154 as a zlib
//   stream, 0.2 per cent). The one-shot helpers store the
//   whole input when the coded stream is bigger than that (as imagedecode's
//   writer always did), so they never expand by more than 5 bytes a 64 KiB
//   block and the framing. A block's type is not chosen by cost (zlib picks the
//   smallest of stored, fixed and dynamic per block): level 1 is always fixed
//   and levels 2 to 9 always dynamic. Left out: a block type by
//   cost, strategies (RLE, Huffman only, fixed), a choice of window
//   size or memory level, and a sync or full flush (a Deflater ends only with
//   'finish').
// - The compressed bytes are fixed by the data and the level: the matcher waits
//   for 262 bytes of look-ahead before it decides a position (258 for the longest
//   match, 3 for the hash of the last position a match covers) unless the input
//   is finished, so cutting the input differently changes nothing.
// - zlib framing: CMF 78 (deflate, 32 KiB window), FLG 01 for stored and level
//   1, 5E for levels 2 to 5, 9C for 6, DA for 7 to 9 (zlib's FLEVEL), FDICT
//   never written. On reading, a window under 32 KiB is
//   accepted and a larger one (CINFO over 7) refused; the stream is not held to
//   the window its header declares (zlib refuses a distance past it). A preset
//   dictionary (FDICT) is refused: BadHeader. The Adler-32 is verified; bytes
//   after it are left alone ('takeRest' has them).
// - gzip framing, writing: ID1 ID2, CM 8, FLG with FTEXT, FEXTRA, FNAME and
//   FCOMMENT as the header says, MTIME (0 for none), XFL (4 at levels 0 and 1,
//   2 at level 9, else 0), OS (255, unknown, unless the header says), the extra field (at most
//   65535 bytes), the name and comment cut at a zero byte (they are ISO 8859-1
//   bytes, zero-terminated); never FHCRC (the header CRC-16). One member per
//   stream. Reading: all of those fields are kept in a GzipHeader; FHCRC is
//   checked when present; reserved flag bits, a method other than 8 and a bad
//   magic are BadHeader; the CRC-32 and ISIZE (the size modulo 2^32) of every
//   member are verified. Members after the first are read and their data joined;
//   zero bytes between members and after the last are padding and ignored (as
//   GNU gzip and Python's gzip module do); any other byte after a member that is
//   not the start of another is TrailingData. A name or comment over 1 MiB is
//   refused. The header (name, comment, extra) is read whole or not at all, so a
//   header cut by the input is waited for.
// - No global and no thread-local. The constant tables of RFC 1951 (lengths,
//   distances, code-length order: the four 'imm' arrays and one const in
//   tables.cone) are moved here from imagedecode unchanged, not added; the CRC
//   table and the Huffman tables are values inside the values that use them.
//   No new module-level table or const was added while the question of
//   whether one is allowed is open: the fixed Huffman codes are built at run time
//   into each Inflater and Deflater's own state, the hash-chain heads are Lists
//   in the Deflater, and even the scalar limits (tokens in a block, look-ahead,
//   the default output cap) are one-line functions, not consts. This costs no
//   measurable speed (a call that returns a literal is inlined); the only
//   module-level names in the package are the five RFC 1951 tables and
//   FastBits, carried over exactly as imagedecode had them.
//   Nothing is shared between two Inflaters or Deflaters, so one per thread or
//   actor is safe; one is not safe to share.
//
// OTHER COPIES. The repository was searched (Cone sources in packages/, samples/,
// tools/, compiler/, 3D Web's packages, and the pegasus3d tree) for another
// private inflate, DEFLATE, CRC-32 or Adler-32: there is none. imagedecode's was
// the only one, and it is gone: the PNG reader and writer call this package.
// (Hits for "inflate" elsewhere are geometry, "inflating" a box.) The scripts
// that make test data (imagedecode's and this package's tests/data/*.py) use
// Python's zlib, as an oracle that no test runs.
//
// NOT HERE: preset dictionaries; DEFLATE64; bzip2, LZMA, brotli (zstd: package zstd); reading
// from a file or a socket (the caller feeds it); a zip or tar container; a
// parallel compressor; the 4 GiB mark of gzip's ISIZE (it is checked modulo 2^32
// and not tested past it).
//
// SPEED AND SIZE of the levels, next to Rust's miniz_oxide 0.9.1 at the same
// level number: 100 MiB of generated text (words, repeated phrases and short
// runs of noise), one swapped run per side, compress time only (the generator's
// 0.31 s taken off), release builds, 13th-gen Core i7-13700HX with the machine
// 40 to 60 per cent busy. The Cone side feeds the Deflater 1 MiB at a time.
//
//             Cone ms   miniz ms   Cone/miniz   Cone size     miniz size
//   level 1       994        401         2.5     33.80 %       34.11 %
//   level 2      2420        632         3.8     26.39 %       26.75 %
//   level 3      3626       1204         3.0     26.07 %       26.05 %
//   level 4      2658       1181         2.3     25.96 %       26.02 %
//   level 5      3262       1528         2.1     25.92 %       25.96 %
//   level 6      4065       2178         1.9     25.89 %       25.90 %
//   level 7      3967       2295         1.7     25.89 %       25.90 %
//   level 8      4266       2358         1.8     25.89 %       25.90 %
//   level 9      3913       2072         1.9     25.89 %       25.90 %
//
// The probe counts and the lazy matching are miniz's, so the sizes match
// closely (levels 4 to 9 are 0.01 to 0.06 points smaller, as a probe here is one
// chain step where miniz's is up to three); the time is the
// byte-at-a-time compare and the bit writer. Both matchers skip a candidate
// without a compare when it differs from the best so far at that length's offset
// (the greedy one, levels 1 to 3, also checks the byte before it and the first two:
// miniz's quick reject; the output is the same). On this text the chain seldom fills
// the longer limits, so levels 6 to 9 cost about the same. Before the levels
// were numbered this way, level 1 probed 16 candidates and entered every
// position of a match (29.7 % of the input, 2.7 s) and level 2, the default,
// probed 48 (26.1 %, 4.8 s).
//
// Inflating runs at 60 to 120 per cent of zlib's speed (measured earlier on
// small files of this repository), the CRC-32 at 1.3 GB/s (zlib's: 1.4 to 2.6)
// and the Adler-32 at 3.8 GB/s (2.4 to 3.2). examples/bench.cone times a file at
// levels 1, 6 and 9. These are measured, not chased: nothing here has been tuned
// for speed past the shape of the algorithms.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   deflate.cone      this overview
//   types.cone        Format, ErrorKind, DeflateError, GzipHeader
//   tables.cone       the constants of RFC 1951
//   checksum.cone     adler32, Adler32, crc32, Crc32
//   huffman.cone      the Huffman decoding table
//   inflate.cone      Inflater, and the gzip header reader
//   decompress.cone   inflate, zlibDecompress, gzipDecompress
//   compress.cone     the bit writer, Huffman code lengths, block writers
//   deflater.cone     Deflater, the matcher, the zlib and gzip headers and trailers
//   compressall.cone  rawDeflate, zlibCompress, gzipCompress, gzipCompressWith

mod deflate;

import collections use *;
import iobuf use IoBuf, Chain;
import streams pub use Status;
